7. Typical Workflow
-------------------

Client creates observers
        |
Registers them with Subject
        |
Subject state changes
        |
notify()
        |
Calls update() on every observer
        |
Observers react

---------------------------------------------------------

8. Advantages
-------------

✔ Loose coupling
✔ Easy to add new observers
✔ Follows Open/Closed Principle
✔ One-to-many communication
✔ Reusable notification mechanism
✔ Dynamic registration/removal of observers

---------------------------------------------------------

9. Disadvantages
----------------

✘ Notification order may not be guaranteed
✘ Too many observers may affect performance
✘ Debugging notification chains can be difficult

---------------------------------------------------------

10. SOLID Principles Used
-------------------------

✔ Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Add new observers without modifying Subject.

✔ Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)
Subject depends on Observer interface, not concrete observers.

---------------------------------------------------------

11. Pull vs Push Model
----------------------

Push Model:
Subject sends all required data.

update(price);

Pull Model:
Subject only notifies.
Observer fetches required data.

update();
observer->getPrice();

---------------------------------------------------------

12. Interview Questions
-----------------------

Q. Why do we need Observer Pattern?

Answer:
To notify multiple dependent objects automatically whenever the state of a
subject changes, without tightly coupling the subject to the observers.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. What problem does Observer solve?

Answer:
It eliminates direct dependencies between the subject and notification
receivers by introducing an Observer interface.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. What is the relationship?

Answer:
One Subject → Many Observers

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. Can observers be added at runtime?

Answer:
Yes.
Observers can be registered and removed dynamically.

---------------------------------------------------------

Q. Which SOLID principles does Observer follow?

Answer:
Open/Closed Principle (OCP)
Dependency Inversion Principle (DIP)

